  "somalia": [
    {
      "title": "Somalia’s ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president - Somali Guardian",
      "link": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-ex-spy-chief-al-shabab-alliance-president/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:23:54 GMT",
      "image": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/8.jpg",
      "content": "Somalia's ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president | Somali Guardian Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Search 28.2 C Mogadishu News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Privacy Policy Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube Somali Guardian News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Home News Somalia Somalia’s ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president Somalia’s ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president October 25, 2026 Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia faces renewed fears of political instability after former intelligence chief Abdullahi Mohamed Ali Sanbalolshe said his Hawadle clan intends to establish a new regional administration in Hiran, raising the prospect of another confrontation with the federal government and intensifying concerns about the country’s fragile political order. In a recent interview with Gaylan Media, Sanbalolshe said the clan had decided to pursue a separate regional administration after being denied the opportunity to secure the presidency of Hirshabelle state under President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud. His remarks went beyond demands for political representation, suggesting that the proposed initiative could develop into an armed resistance movement against the government in Mogadishu. Sanbalolshe warned that Somalia was approaching a dangerous turning point, predicting a major war and arguing that the current political order was weakening. He said his clan wanted to secure an influential position in whatever political system emerged from the turmoil. The comments come amid separate warnings from Somali counterterrorism official Abdirahman Turyare, who recently alleged that plans were being developed to establish armed rebellions in Hiran and Middle Shabelle, potentially drawing the two regions into a wider confrontation with federal authorities. Sanbalolshe raises prospect of armed rebellion Sanbalolshe indicated that his movement would be prepared to take significant risks to remove Hassan Sheikh Mohamud’s government, suggesting that it would seek assistance from any party willing to support that objective. Asked whether his group would cooperate with Al-Shabaab, the Islamist militant organization fighting the Somali government, Sanbalolshe did not rule out the possibility. His remarks raise the prospect of a particularly volatile situation in which opponents of the federal government might consider tactical cooperation with a militant organization responsible for years of attacks against Somali security forces and officials. However, the interview account does not establish that Sanbalolshe or his associates have reached an agreement with Al-Shabaab, nor does it independently confirm that a coordinated armed campaign is underway. Sanbalolshe also said his movement would do worse than Southwest State leader Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen, who has faced accusations of coordinating with Al-Shabaab during clashes with federal security forces in Baidoa and other major towns in Bay region. Sanbalolshe’s comparison nevertheless underscores the severity of the confrontation he appears to envisage if the dispute over regional power escalates into armed conflict. UAE meetings raise questions about regional involvement Sanbalolshe said he had recently travelled to the United Arab Emirates, where he met Somaliland President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi, known as Irro; Puntland leader Said Abdullahi Deni; and Jubaland leader Ahmed Mohamed Islam, widely known as Ahmed Madobe. According to Sanbalolshe’s account, he urged the leaders to support an armed rebellion against the federal government in the Hiran region. It remains unclear whether any of the leaders agreed to his proposal or whether the reported meetings resulted in concrete political or military commitments. The reported outreach is significant because Somaliland, Puntland and Jubaland occupy distinct positions in Somalia’s contested political landscape. Any involvement by their leaders in a campaign against the federal government could deepen existing divisions between Mogadishu and regional authorities. Sanbalolshe also said the proposed movement would seek assistance from neighbouring Ethiopia, potentially extending the dispute beyond Somalia’s domestic political arena. The reported discussions and proposed external support have not been independently verified. No conclusion can be drawn from Sanbalolshe’s statements alone about the involvement of foreign governments or regional leaders in plans for an armed rebellion. Nevertheless, the prospect of outside support for a new anti-government movement could complicate efforts to contain tensions and maintain cooperation among Somalia’s political and security institutions. Counterterrorism official warns of two planned rebellions Sanbalolshe’s comments come just days after Somali counterterrorism official Abdirahman Turyare warned that plans were being developed to establish two armed rebellions in Hiran and Middle Shabelle. Turyare alleged that the proposed movements were being financed by the United Arab Emirates and intended to cooperate with Al-Shabaab in a campaign against the federal government. He urged the authorities to intervene as early as possible to prevent the alleged plans from materialising. The timing of the two sets of remarks is likely to intensify scrutiny of the security situation in central Somalia. It is also unclear whether the alleged rebellions described by Turyare are directly connected to the initiative outlined by Sanbalolshe, or whether the two statements refer to the same organized effort. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Previous article Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official Somaliguardian news RELATED ARTICLES MORE FROM AUTHOR Somalia Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official Somalia Somalia sends more than 200 military vehicles toward Burhakabo Somalia Somalia opposition scores own goal over Galmudug election Somalia Somalia: Key Laftagaren ally survives alleged Turkish drone strike Somalia Somalia’s opposition launches first court challenge to new constitution Somalia Somalia parliament speaker accuses Kenya of harbouring terrorists Somalia Somalia angry as Ethiopia refuses to intervene in South West State conflict Somalia Somalia police enter Hormuud headquarters in Mogadishu amid Southwest state tensions Somalia Somalia president rejects opposition calls for consensus-based election Somalia Somalia’s government loses all major Bay towns but Baidoa to Laftagaren Somalia Somalia’s government loses Burhakabo to ousted Southwest leader’s forces Somalia Somalia agrees to buy Pakistani JF-17 fighters ABOUT US Somaliguardian is an East African leading news website. We are impartial and independent, every day we create distinctive, world class content which inform and educate thousands of people in Somalia, East Africa and around the world. Get updated with our real time news coverage from Somalia, Africa and the entire world. FOLLOW US Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Privacy Policy © 2021 Somaliguardian error: Content is protected!",
      "summary": "Somalia's political landscape is facing renewed instability as former intelligence chief Abdullahi Mohamed Ali Sanbalolshe announced his Hawadle clan's intention to establish a new regional administration in Hiran. This move follows their dissatisfaction with the federal government's handling of political representation, particularly regarding the presidency of Hirshabelle state. Sanbalolshe's comments suggest a potential shift towards armed resistance against President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud's government, raising fears of a major conflict. He did not dismiss the possibility of collaborating with Al-Shabaab, the militant group opposing the government, which could lead to a dangerous alliance. Additionally, Sanbalolshe has sought support from regional leaders in the UAE for an armed rebellion, although it remains unclear if any agreements were reached. Concurrently, Somali counterterrorism officials have warned of planned rebellions in Hiran and Middle Shabelle, allegedly financed by the UAE, further complicating the already fragile political situation in Somalia.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/somalia-s-ex-spy-chief-does-not-rule-out-al-shabaab-alliance-911d727635.html"
    },
    {
      "title": "Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army - FTL Somalia",
      "link": "https://www.ftlsomalia.com/lawmakers-confront-defence-ministry-over-alleged-rights-violations-in-the-army/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:53:19 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/MoD-Parliament-Committee.jpeg",
      "content": "Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Politics Nationwide Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army October 25, 2026 4 min read MOGADISHU , The Defence Committee of the House of the People held an accountability session with Defence Minister Ahmed Moallim Fiqi on Sunday, pressing the ministry on salary deductions affecting soldiers, the rights of orphans and disabled veterans, and the persistent challenges undermining the welfare of the armed forces. The meeting, chaired by Acting Committee Chairman MP Abdirazak Hassan Dhookir, was attended by the Minister, Ministry’s Permanent Secretary, the Director General, and other senior officials. The committee emphasized that it is unacceptable for the rights and salaries of soldiers who sacrificed their lives for the country’s defence, as well as the benefits owed to orphans and disabled veterans, to be reduced without full consideration of their circumstances and entitlements. Discussions also covered the ongoing campaign against Al-Shabaab , the construction and professionalization of the Somali National Army, the imperative of keeping the military out of politics, the prevention of the misuse of security forces, and the long-standing challenges related to military ranks and promotions that require a durable solution. A Pattern of Oversight The session is the latest in a series of engagements between the Defence Committee and the Ministry, reflecting a broader push by Parliament to assert its oversight role over the security sector. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the committee’s earlier work , the Defence Committee has been actively involved in shaping the draft National Defence Bill, incorporating recommendations from military commanders and pressing for reforms in military structure, command authority, and accountability. The committee has also raised concerns about the promotion system within the military, with lawmakers criticizing the current process as flawed and demanding correction within the legislation. Deputy Minister of Defence Omar Ali Abdi has previously stated that no soldier in the national forces goes unpaid, pledging accountability if that were proven otherwise. The Broader Context of Military Reform The issues raised in Sunday’s meeting are not new. Salary deductions and delays have been a persistent grievance within the Somali National Army, particularly for soldiers deployed in frontline units. The welfare of orphans and disabled veterans has also been a long-standing concern, with advocacy groups repeatedly calling for a functioning pension and benefits system. These challenges are compounded by the broader transition process. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the security transition , the Somali National Army is preparing to assume full responsibility for the country’s security as the African Union mission draws down. The AUSSOM mandate is set to expire at the end of 2026, and international partners are pressing Mogadishu to build a self-sufficient, professional force capable of sustaining operations without external support. That transition will require not only training and equipment but also the kind of institutional stability that ensures soldiers are paid on time, veterans are cared for, and the army is insulated from political interference. The Defence Committee’s emphasis on these issues reflects a recognition that military effectiveness is inseparable from the welfare and dignity of those who serve. Critical Note The Defence Committee’s meeting with Minister Fiqi is a welcome exercise of parliamentary oversight, and the issues it raised are legitimate. Soldiers who risk their lives for the state deserve to be paid on time and in full. The orphans and disabled veterans of the armed forces deserve the benefits that were promised to them. The committee’s insistence that these rights cannot be reduced without consideration is a necessary corrective to a system that has often treated military personnel as an afterthought. But accountability sessions have become a recurring feature of Somali politics, and their impact depends on follow-through. The committee and the ministry agreed to continue their meetings and to strengthen transparency in the Ministry of Defence and the Somali National Army. Those are the right commitments. Whether they produce concrete changes in the lives of soldiers and veterans will depend on whether the committee is willing to escalate its pressure if the ministry fails to deliver. The meeting also highlighted a tension that the government has not fully resolved. The committee warned against political interference in the military and the misuse of security forces. That warning comes at a time when the federal government stands accused, by opposition figures and regional leaders, of deploying the army to settle political disputes in states like South West. The Defence Committee’s words are a reminder that civilian control of the military is not just a principle to be proclaimed. It is a practice that must be protected, even when it is inconvenient for the government of the day. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Parliament Defence Committee Incorporates Military Commanders’ Input into National Defence Bill Somali Army Chief Reviews Security Transition Defence Minister Fiqi Meets Italian Military Delegation Led by Gen. Iannucci Somali Army Chief Welcomes Italian Military Team for Defense Talks Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Former NISA Chief Alleges Abu Dhabi Plot with Deni, Madobe and Cirro as Mogadishu Escalates UAE Accusation Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process You may also like Politics Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process October 25, 2026 5 min read Politics Somali Ministers Call for Dialogue as Somaliland Elders Attend Mogadishu Event October 23, 2026 4 min read Politics Somali PM Hails Women’s Role in Nation-Building at Islamic Scholars Conference October 22, 2026 5 min read Latest News Former NISA Chief Alleges Abu Dhabi Plot with Deni, Madobe and Cirro as Mogadishu Escalates UAE Accusation Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint BECO, Mogadishu Power and Blue Sky Announce Rate Increase Amid 70% Fuel Spike Turkish Space Agency Delegation Visits Mogadishu and Warsheikh as Defence Cooperation Expands Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow Hirshabelle Victory Celebrated in Mogadishu as Political Consensus Remains Elusive Oman’s Embassy Reopens in Mogadishu as Al-Umairi Presents Credentials Abiy Ahmed Calls Sea Access an Existential Issue for Ethiopia’s 130 Million People Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "Lawmakers in Somalia's Defence Committee held a session with Defence Minister Ahmed Moallim Fiqi to address concerns over alleged rights violations within the army, including salary deductions for soldiers and the welfare of orphans and disabled veterans. The meeting, led by Acting Committee Chairman MP Abdirazak Hassan Dhookir, highlighted the unacceptable treatment of military personnel who have sacrificed for the nation. The committee also discussed the ongoing fight against Al-Shabaab and the need for military professionalism, emphasizing the importance of keeping the armed forces free from political interference. This session is part of a broader parliamentary effort to oversee the security sector and push for reforms, including a review of the flawed military promotion system. While the Defence Ministry has pledged accountability, the effectiveness of these discussions will depend on the committee's ability to ensure follow-through on commitments made to improve the conditions for soldiers and veterans.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/lawmakers-confront-defence-ministry-over-alleged-rights-viol-7ba66939f6.html"
    },
    {
      "title": "Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint - FTL Somalia",
      "link": "https://www.ftlsomalia.com/turkish-envoy-alkan-meets-police-chief-as-ankara-expands-security-footprint/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:18:16 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Turkish-Ambassador-Somali-Police.jpg",
      "content": "Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Security Nationwide Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint October 25, 2026 4 min read MOGADISHU , A high-level Turkish delegation led by Ambassador Ferhat Alkan visited the headquarters of the Somali Police Force on Sunday, meeting with Police Commissioner Brigadier General Asad Osman Abdullahi to reaffirm Ankara’s commitment to training and equipping Somalia’s elite Haramcad special police unit. According to a statement from the Turkish Embassy in Mogadishu, Ambassador Alkan was accompanied by Murat Daş, a Consultant Special Operations Security Director, and Commissioner Emre Dikyol, a Police Liaison Officer from the Foreign Relations Department of the Turkish General Directorate of Security, along with members of the Turkish Police Special Operations. The embassy described Turkey’s Police Special Operations Directorate as “one of Turkey’s most elite units” and confirmed that its training activities aimed at strengthening the Haramcad Special Police Forces continue “uninterrupted.” The statement also noted that Turkey’s Ministry of Interior, General Directorate of Security, Gendarmerie General Command, and Coast Guard Command are all continuing security cooperation projects with Somalia. This is not a new relationship. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the police partnership , the Somali Police Chief met with a Turkish delegation in May 2026 to discuss expanding training programs specifically for the Haramcad unit, which has received extensive Turkish support through training, equipment, and modernization. The Haramcad commandos have graduated from the TURKSOM Military Academy in Mogadishu, where they were trained in high-risk operational tactics, rapid response, and civilian protection, as detailed in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the graduation ceremonies . The Turkish Ambassador at the time, Alper Aktaş, stated in August 2026 that Turkey had trained more than 1,000 specialized personnel within the Somali Police Force, in addition to the Gorgor special forces. A Broader Diplomatic Tour Ambassador Alkan’s presence at the police headquarters is consistent with the pattern he has established since presenting his credentials to President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud in late September. He has met with Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre to discuss energy, infrastructure, fisheries, security, health, education, space, and connectivity, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the talks with the Prime Minister . He has visited the spaceport under construction in Warsheikh. He has engaged with the Ministry of Information and met with Somali youth at a Mogadishu café founded by former students in Turkey. The police visit, however, carries particular weight. The Haramcad unit is not a conventional police force. It is a specialized commando unit that has been at the center of Turkey’s security assistance to Somalia, alongside the Gorgor military commandos. Turkey’s investment in these units reflects a broader strategy of building elite, Turkish-trained forces that can operate alongside or independently of the larger, less cohesive Somali National Army. Critical Note The Turkish Embassy’s statement is nothing out of the ordinary, but the presence of a “Consultant Special Operations Security Director” and a police liaison officer from Ankara’s General Directorate of Security, rather than a military attaché, underscores that this is a police-to-police relationship, distinct from Turkey’s military training mission at TURKSOM. The emphasis on “uninterrupted training activities” is also significant. In a security environment where international partners frequently suspend or condition their support based on political developments, Turkey’s commitment to the Haramcad unit has been consistent. That consistency has made Ankara an indispensable partner for the Somali Police Force, but it has also given Turkey a degree of influence over a unit that operates in Somalia’s most sensitive security spaces. For Somalia, the Haramcad model offers a template for building capable, professional forces. But the unit’s effectiveness ultimately depends on its integration into a broader security architecture that includes the police, the military, and the justice system. A well-trained commando unit operating without accountability mechanisms or a functioning criminal justice system is a tool that can protect civilians or intimidate them, depending on who is giving the orders. The Turkish Embassy’s statement thanks the Somali Police Force for its hospitality. The more important question is whether the partnership it describes is building a police force that serves the public, or one that serves the government of the day. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Somali Police Chief Discusses Security Cooperation with Turkish Delegation Police Chief Graduates Haramcad Commandos at TURKSOM Academy Turkish Ambassador Continues Diplomatic Tour: Discuss with PM Hamza Energy, Security and Space Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants You may also like Security Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants October 25, 2026 6 min read Security Min. Fiqi Thanks EU Amb. Máthé for Security Support as Mogadishu Pushes for Faster International Cooperation October 24, 2026 5 min read Security Deadly Oromo-Somali Clashes in Moyale Kill Nine, Displace Thousands to Kenya October 24, 2026 5 min read Latest News Former NISA Chief Alleges Abu Dhabi Plot with Deni, Madobe and Cirro as Mogadishu Escalates UAE Accusation Lawmakers Confront Defence Ministry Over Alleged Rights Violations in the Army Turkish Envoy Alkan Meets Police Chief as Ankara Expands Security Footprint BECO, Mogadishu Power and Blue Sky Announce Rate Increase Amid 70% Fuel Spike Turkish Space Agency Delegation Visits Mogadishu and Warsheikh as Defence Cooperation Expands Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow Hirshabelle Victory Celebrated in Mogadishu as Political Consensus Remains Elusive Oman’s Embassy Reopens in Mogadishu as Al-Umairi Presents Credentials Abiy Ahmed Calls Sea Access an Existential Issue for Ethiopia’s 130 Million People Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "Turkish Ambassador Ferhat Alkan recently met with Somali Police Commissioner Brigadier General Asad Osman Abdullahi to reinforce Turkey's commitment to training Somalia's elite Haramcad special police unit. This meeting, part of a high-level Turkish delegation's visit to the Somali Police Force headquarters, emphasized ongoing security cooperation between Turkey and Somalia. The Turkish Embassy highlighted that training activities for the Haramcad unit, which specializes in high-risk operations, are continuing without interruption. Turkey has previously trained over 1,000 personnel within the Somali Police Force, reflecting a broader strategy to develop elite forces capable of operating independently of the Somali National Army. Ambassador Alkan's visit aligns with his diplomatic efforts since taking office, including discussions on various sectors such as energy and health. However, the effectiveness of the Haramcad unit hinges on its integration within a comprehensive security framework, raising concerns about accountability and the potential for misuse of power.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/turkish-envoy-alkan-meets-police-chief-as-ankara-expands-sec-b9f37e37eb.html"
